<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Section 2.4 - Linear independence Part 2</title>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the matrices
                <md>
                    A = 
  \left[
    \begin{array}{rrrr}
      3 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 1 \\
      1 \amp -1 \amp 3 \amp 7 \\
      3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 5 \\
      -1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\
    \end{array}
  \right] \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, 
  B = 
  \left[
    \begin{array}{rrrr}
      3 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 4 \\
      1 \amp -1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \\
      3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
      -1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
    \end{array}
  \right].
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Are the columns of <m>A</m> linearly independent?
                    Are the columns of <m>B</m> linearly independent?
                    Explain your thinking.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    <md>
                        A = 
  \left[
    \begin{array}{rrrr}
      3 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 1 \\
      1 \amp -1 \amp 3 \amp 7 \\
      3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 5 \\
      -1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\
    \end{array}
  \right] \sim 
  \left[
    \begin{array}{rrrr}
      1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
      0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
    \end{array}
  \right].
                    </md>
                    Therefore, the columns of <m>A</m> are not linearly
                    independent because there isn't a pivot in every column.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <md>
                        B =                
  \left[
    \begin{array}{rrrr}
      3 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 4 \\
      1 \amp -1 \amp 3 \amp -1 \\
      3 \amp -2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
      -1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
    \end{array}
  \right] \sim \left[
    \begin{array}{rrrr}
      1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
      0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
      0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
    \end{array}
  \right].
                    </md>
                    Therefore, the columns of <m>B</m> are linearly independent
                    because there is a pivot in every column.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1.25in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For the matrix above whose columns are linearly dependent,
                    express one of the vectors as a linear combination of the
                    others.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    Since <m>A</m> is the matrix whose columns are linearly
                    dependent, we can write one of those columns as a linear
                    combination of the others.
                    Using the RREF from part (a), we have that the last column
                    is the coefficients of the first three vectors that will
                    create a linear combination equalling the last vector.
                    So, <m>{\bvec_4 = \bvec_1 + 0\bvec_2 + 2\bvec_3}</m>, where
                    <m>\bvec_i</m> is the ith column of the matrix <m>B</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <task workspace="0.9in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain why seven vectors in <m>\real^5</m> cannot be
                    linearly independent.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    If we set up the vectors as columns of a matrix and row
                    reduce, the maximum number of pivots is 5 because there are
                    only 5 rows (corresponding to the number of entries in each
                    vector).
                    This leaves 2 columns without a pivot, thus indicating
                    linear dependence!
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.9in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What is the largest number of vectors in <m>\real^5</m> that
                    can be linearly independent?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    The largest number of linearly independent vectors is 5!
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.9in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If you have three vectors in <m>\real^5</m>, can you
                    guarantee that they are linearly independent?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    There is no general guarantee that any three random vectors
                    in <m>\mathbb{R}^5</m> are linearly independent.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>\vvec_1, \vvec_2, \ldots, \vvec_n</m> are
                vectors in <m>\real^{24}</m> that are linearly independent and
                that span <m>\real^{24}</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What can you say about the pivot positions of the matrix
                    <m>A</m> whose columns are
                    <m>\vvec_1, \vvec_2, \ldots, \vvec_n</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    The matrix <m>A</m> has a pivot in every column.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    How many vectors are there in this set?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    There are 24 vectors in this set!
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>\bvec</m> is a vector in <m>\real^{24}</m>.
                    Explain how you know that <m>\bvec</m> can be written as a
                    linear combination of
                    <m>\vvec_1,\vvec_2, \ldots,\vvec_n</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    If there are 24 vectors in <m>\real^{24}</m>, this means
                    that the matrix <m>A</m> is square (size
                    <m>{24\times 24}</m> ).
                    With a pivot in every column of <m>A</m>, the system
                    <m>{[A | \bvec]}</m> is always consistent for any
                    <m>\bvec</m> and thus, <m>\bvec</m> can be written as a
                    linear combination of
                    <m>{\vvec_1,\vvec_2, \ldots,\vvec_n}</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that <m>\bvec</m> is a vector in <m>\real^{24}</m>.
                    Explain how you know that <m>\bvec</m> can be written as a
                    linear combination of <m>\vvec_1,\vvec_2, \ldots,\vvec_n</m>
                    in exactly one way.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    Connected to the previous answer, since the system
                    <m>{[A | \bvec]}</m> is always consistent with a pivot in
                    any column, the solution will be unique for each
                    <m>\bvec</m>.
                    This means that <m>\bvec</m> can be written as a linear
                    combination of <m>{\vvec_1,\vvec_2, \ldots,\vvec_n}</m> in
                    exactly one way.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What can you say about the solution to the homogeneous
                    equation <m>{A\xvec = \zerovec}</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    The unique solution to this system is
                    <m>{\xvec = \zerovec}</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\ldots,\vvec_n</m> are a set of
                vectors and that <m>\vvec_3</m> is a scalar multiple of
                <m>\vvec_7</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What can you say about the linear independence/dependence of
                    this set of vectors?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    This set of vectors is linearly dependent since
                    <m>\vvec_3</m> can be written as a linear combination of
                    <m>\vvec_7</m> and are both in the set.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\ldots,\vvec_n</m> is a linearly
                    independent set of vectors, is it necessarily true that one
                    vector is a scalar multiple of another one?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    It can't be true that one vector is a scalar multiple of
                    another one for this set to be linearly independent.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>A</m> is a matrix such that the equation
                <m>{A\xvec=\bvec}</m> has exactly one solution for every vector
                <m>\bvec</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What can you say about the linear dependence/independence of
                    the columns of <m>A</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    We can say that the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly
                    independent because there will be a pivot in every column.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What can you say about the span of the columns of <m>A</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="11-solutions">
                <p>
                    If <m>A</m> is an <m>m\times n</m> matrix and there is
                    exactly one solution for every vector <m>\bvec</m>, then
                    there is a pivot in every row.
                    This then means that the span of the columns is equal to
                    <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>.
                    Note that <m>n</m> must be equal or larger than <m>m</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <outcomes>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    What does it mean for a set of vectors to be linearly
                    dependent?
                    Linearly independent?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What can you say about the pivots of a matrix if the columns
                    of that matrix are a linearly dependent set of vectors?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    What is the maximum number of vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^m</m>
                    that can be linearly dependent?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    When there is a non-trivial solution to the homogeneous
                    equation <m>A\xvec=\zerovec</m>, what can you say about the
                    columns of the matrix <m>A</m> (in terms of linear
                    dependence/independence)?
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </outcomes>
</worksheet>